\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces Schematic of PINN training. (1) Collocation points are fed into the neural network. (2) Automatic differentiation yields spatial and temporal derivatives. (3) PDE residuals—and hence the loss terms—are evaluated at those points. (4) The resulting losses are combined to form $\mathcal {L}(\theta )$; its gradient drives an optimization step on~$\theta $. The cycle repeats until convergence.}}{20}{figure.caption.7}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces Collocation points for a put option. Points are sampled inside the domain~$\Omega $, on the boundary~$\partial \Omega $, and on the initial slice $\Omega _0$.}}{22}{figure.caption.8}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Call option price under the Black-Scholes PDE. The PINN solution closely matches the analytical benchmark.}}{24}{figure.caption.10}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces Absolute error of the PINN solution. Errors peak close to maturity and the strike price.}}{25}{figure.caption.11}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces Option value at expiry. Away from the strike, the PINN matches the analytical solution; near the kink the absolute error peaks.}}{25}{figure.caption.12}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.4}{\ignorespaces $\Delta $ at different times. Small oscillations emerge only very close to $t=T$.}}{26}{figure.caption.13}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.5}{\ignorespaces $\Gamma $ at different times. Oscillations near the strike dominate the error; a secondary mismatch appears for very small $S$.}}{27}{figure.caption.14}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.6}{\ignorespaces $\Theta $ at different times. The main discrepancies occur both near inception and as $t \to T$.}}{27}{figure.caption.15}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.7}{\ignorespaces Absolute error of the PINN solution. The error is concentrated around the strike price and maturity.}}{28}{figure.caption.17}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.8}{\ignorespaces Dimensionless multi-asset best-of European option price (first two asset axes). The red circle highlights the region where the solution is less consistent with the expected behaviour.}}{29}{figure.caption.19}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.9}{\ignorespaces Multi-asset best-of European option price in original coordinates (first two asset axes). The red circle highlights the region where the solution is less consistent with the expected behaviour. This artifact is amplified as re-scaling is applied.}}{30}{figure.caption.20}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.10}{\ignorespaces Seven-asset best-of option surfaces for two architectures. The network with 60 neurons per layer captures the solution more faithfully than the smaller 10-neuron model.}}{31}{figure.caption.21}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.11}{\ignorespaces Training loss for multi-asset PINNs. Higher dimensions slow convergence and flatten the loss curve. The smaller network sometimes terminates early, yielding a shorter training time but a noticeably larger error.}}{32}{figure.caption.22}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.12}{\ignorespaces CPU timing comparison between PINN inference and Monte Carlo simulation (50000 simulations).}}{32}{figure.caption.23}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.13}{\ignorespaces Call option price surfaces obtained with the Heston-Hull-White model for different volatility levels.}}{34}{figure.caption.25}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.14}{\ignorespaces Difference between Heston-Hull-White and Black-Scholes call prices as a function of volatility. Mean reversion in the Heston model produces the characteristic bell shape.}}{35}{figure.caption.26}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.15}{\ignorespaces Difference between Heston-Hull-White and Black-Scholes call prices as a function of the short rate. Stochastic mean reversion of rates leads to systematically higher prices than under constant-rate discounting, particularly when initial rates are low.}}{35}{figure.caption.27}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.16}{\ignorespaces European put option pricing: comparison between PINN (left) and analytical Black-Scholes solution (right). Notice that the stock price axis is reversed compared to the call option.}}{37}{figure.caption.29}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.17}{\ignorespaces Absolute error distribution for the European put option. Errors peak around maturity near the strike price.}}{37}{figure.caption.30}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.18}{\ignorespaces Put-call parity verification: the difference between the PINN solutions for call and put options matches the expected relation.}}{38}{figure.caption.31}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.19}{\ignorespaces American put option prices: PINN solution (left) compared to a numerical benchmark solution (right).}}{39}{figure.caption.32}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.20}{\ignorespaces Absolute error for American put option pricing. It is possible to observe that the PINN is able to reproduce closely most of the free boundary.}}{40}{figure.caption.33}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.21}{\ignorespaces European payer swaption pricing surface. Left: PINN solution. Right: reference solution computed with an analytic approximation or semi-analytical method.}}{41}{figure.caption.34}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.22}{\ignorespaces Absolute error in pricing the European payer swaption. The error is primarily concentrated near expiry and around the strike.}}{42}{figure.caption.35}%
